<h2>What is the Stock Market?</h2>
<section class="lesson-card">

    <h3> The Basics </h3>
    <p>
        The stock market is a marketplace where people can buy and sell ownership in companies.
        When you hear about companies like Apple, Microsoft, or Tesla being traded, this happens
        on stock exchanges such as the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) or Nasdaq.
    </p>

    <p>
        Companies use the stock market to raise money. By selling shares of their company to the
        public, they receive capital that can be used to grow the business, develop new products,
        or expand into new markets.
    </p>

    <p>
        Investors, on the other hand, buy these shares with the hope that the company will grow
        and become more valuable over time.
    </p>

    <h4>What is a Stock?</h4>

    <p>
        A stock represents a small piece of ownership in a company. When you buy a stock, you
        become a shareholder, meaning you own a fraction of that business.
    </p>

    <p>
        If the company performs well and becomes more profitable, the value of the stock may
        increase. Investors can then sell their shares for a profit. Some companies also pay
        dividends, which are regular payments made to shareholders.
    </p>

    <h4>What is a Fund?</h4>

    <p>
        A fund is a collection of many different investments grouped together into a single
        product. Instead of buying shares in just one company, a fund allows you to invest in
        dozens or even hundreds of companies at the same time.
    </p>

    <p>
        Funds are popular because they provide diversification. This means your investment risk
        is spread across many companies instead of relying on the success of just one.
    </p>

    <h4>Why Do People Invest?</h4>

    <p>
        The main reason people invest is to grow their money over time. Historically, the stock
        market has provided higher long-term returns than keeping money in a traditional savings
        account.
    </p>

    <p>
        However, investing also involves risk. Prices can go up and down in the short term, which
        is why long-term investing and diversification are important strategies.
    </p>
</section>
<br />

<h2> Stocks </h2>
<section class="lesson-card">

    <h3> How Stocks Work in Practice </h3>
    <p>
        A stock represents ownership in a company. When you buy a stock, you are purchasing a small
        share of that business. This means that if the company grows and becomes more valuable,
        the value of your share can increase as well.
    </p>

    <p>
        Stocks are traded on stock exchanges such as the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) or Nasdaq.
        However, most investors do not buy stocks directly from the exchange. Instead, they use a
        brokerage platform, which is a service that allows individuals to buy and sell stocks online.
    </p>

    <h4> Buying a Stock </h4>

    <p>
        When you decide to buy a stock, you place an order through your broker. The broker then
        matches your order with someone who is willing to sell that stock. Once the order is
        executed, you officially become a shareholder in the company.
    </p>

    <p>
        For example, if a company's stock is trading at $100 and you buy one share, you now own a
        small portion of that company worth $100 at that moment. If the price later increases to
        $120, your share has gained value.
    </p>

    <h4> Selling a Stock </h4>

    <p>
        Selling a stock works in the opposite way. When you sell your shares, you place a sell
        order through your broker. Another investor who wants to buy the stock will purchase
        it from you at the current market price.
    </p>

    <p>
        If you bought a stock for $100 and later sell it for $120, you make a profit of $20.
        If you sell it for less than you paid, you take a loss instead.
    </p>

    <h4> Why Stock Prices Change </h4>

    <p>
        Stock prices constantly move because of supply and demand. When many investors want to
        buy a stock, the price tends to go up. When more investors want to sell than buy, the
        price usually goes down.
    </p>

    <p>
        Many factors can influence stock prices, including company performance, economic news,
        interest rates, and investor expectations about the future.
    </p>

    <p>
        Because of these fluctuations, investing in stocks can be volatile in the short term.
        This is why many investors focus on long-term investing rather than trying to predict
        short-term price movements.
    </p>
</section>
