<h2>Funds</h2>
<section class="lesson-card">


    <h3>What is a Fund?</h3>

    <p>
        A fund is an investment product that pools money from many investors and uses that
        money to buy a collection of different assets. These assets often include stocks,
        but they can also include bonds or other financial instruments. Instead of buying
        shares in a single company, an investor who buys a fund gains exposure to many
        companies at the same time.
    </p>

    <p>
        Funds are usually managed by professional portfolio managers who decide which
        companies or assets the fund should invest in. Their goal is typically to follow
        a certain investment strategy, such as tracking a market index or investing in
        companies within a specific sector.
    </p>

    <h4>How Funds Differ from Stocks</h4>

    <p>
        The main difference between a stock and a fund is diversification. When you buy a
        single stock, your investment depends entirely on the performance of that one
        company. If the company performs poorly, the value of your investment may decline
        significantly.
    </p>

    <p>
        With a fund, your money is spread across many companies. This means that the
        performance of a single company has a smaller impact on your total investment.
        Because of this diversification, funds are generally considered less risky than
        investing in individual stocks.
    </p>

    <h4>Risk and Return</h4>

    <p>
        Although funds typically carry lower risk than individual stocks, they are still
        affected by movements in the overall market. If the stock market declines, the
        value of many funds will also decrease.
    </p>

    <p>
        However, diversification helps reduce the impact of individual company failures.
        For many beginner investors, funds can be a simple way to participate in the
        stock market without needing to analyze and select individual companies.
    </p>

    <h4>Why Many Investors Choose Funds</h4>

    <p>
        Funds are popular because they provide diversification, professional management,
        and convenience. Instead of researching and managing dozens of individual
        investments, investors can buy a single fund that already contains a diversified
        portfolio.
    </p>

    <p>
        Many long‑term investors combine funds and stocks in their portfolios. Funds can
        provide stability and diversification, while individual stocks may offer higher
        growth potential but also higher risk.
    </p>
</section>